> Regular and systematic monitoring of coral reef habitat is an essential tool in reef management, conservation and restoration. However, traditional monitoring techniques, which involve manual collection and analysis of imagery, are spatially limited and labor-intensive. Consequently, few coral reef ecosystems are regularly and comprehensively surveyed, and most existing records suffer spatial and temporal gaps that can skew interpretation of variability and trends. Advances in marine robotics and more recently, in computer vision, offer scalable alternatives by reducing demand on human practitioners. Here, we present a modular machine learning pipeline for large-scale segmentation and classification of reef imagery, demonstrated on a case study of top-down images collected by an unmanned surface vehicle (USV) in Majuro, Marshall Islands during an active bleaching event in October 2024. We show for the first time how semantic segmentation of underwater survey imagery can be used to measure both live and bleached coral cover for different coral taxonomic groups. The approach integrates the Segment Anything Model~2 for mask proposal generation with a merging procedure tuned to balance recall and precision across coral size scales, followed by convolutional neural networks (CNNs) that filter out non-coral instances and classify coral morpho-taxonomic class and bleaching status. On the held-out test set of 111 images, the full pipeline attains a live coral cover Intersection-over-Union of 0.674 and Dice coefficient of 0.781 against expert ground truth, outperforming a competing coral segmentation framework, CoralSCOP, on the same images. At the mask level, once a candidate mask has been proposed, the classification ensemble attains 90.3\% accuracy, 94.6\% recall, and 93.0\% precision on an out-of-sample coral morpho-taxonomic and bleaching-status classification. These results demonstrate the feasibility of automated coral health assessment on real-world survey data, enabling large-scale monitoring of reefs during bleaching events to study drivers of coral resilience to heat stress.
