< One of the key challenges faced by stakeholders attempting to mitigate the rapid loss of coral reef ecosystems is the ability to efficiently monitor reefs, collect coral census data, and track the severity and extent of bleaching events. Coral reefs represent the most biodiverse ecosystems in our oceans---despite covering less than 1\% of the seafloor, they are home to over 25\% of all marine species \citep{reakakudla1997}. They provide ecosystem services valued upwards of trillions of dollars through fisheries, tourism, and coastal protection, and directly support the livelihoods of billions of people across the globe \citep{costanza2014,sing2022}. However, these invaluable communities are in crisis across the globe: a majority of reefs are faced with local stressors like overfishing and land-based sources of pollution, many regions have been impacted by large-scale outbreaks of disease, and corals all over the world are reeling from the global scale threat of coral bleaching in response to climate change \citep{burke2011reefs,vega2025, hughes2017}. The mass bleaching of 2024 was the Fourth Global Mass Bleaching Event, affecting 84\% of all reefs on earth, and mortality in many areas approaching 100\% \cite{spady2025,birkart2025}. Coral reef stakeholders---a title that applies to the 13\% of the planet's population who live near coral reefs---have deployed a wide range of local interventions to attempt to address these threats, from the establishment of MPA networks, active coral restoration efforts, the identification and cultivation of thermally tolerant corals, and more \citep{strain2019, bostrom2020}. But collecting data to guide the implementation of these tools and quantify their long-term success is difficult. While large monitoring efforts have been successful in capturing spatial patterns in coral bleaching \citep{winston2022,rowell2025}, the resource-intensity of these types of programs limits their transferability to coral reefs worldwide. This scarcity of high-resolution, long-term, and spatially extensive data on patterns of coral bleaching has been a limitation to advancing our understanding of both the interaction of heat stress intensity and duration that causes coral bleaching and of environmental conditions that may promote a reef's resilience to heat stress.
---
