< As machine learning techniques for image analysis have advanced, several different approaches have been applied to the task of classification of underwater coral reef imagery. Beijbom et al. used a support vector machine model (SVM) to classify sampled points within an image, with prediction accuracy reaching 70-80\% \citep{beijbom2012}. This approach, made publicly available in the online tool CoralNet, essentially automated commonly used random or stratified point sampling methods (e.g. Coral Point Count \citep{kohlergill2006}). Platforms such as ReefCloud and MERMAID similarly apply CNNs to point-annotated reef imagery for scalable benthic cover estimation, and provide helpful GUIs that aim to bring these models into the operations of users around the world \citep{gonzalezrivero2020,mermaid2026}. Point-based approaches efficiently estimate percent cover, but cannot recover colony-level size, shape, or per-colony bleaching status that full semantic segmentation provides. Training of semantic segmentation models, however, requires large amounts of densely labeled data. Alonso et al. \citep{alonso2017,alonso2019} used a superpixel segmentation algorithm to augment the sparsely annotated labels generated by Coral Point Count and CoralNet into densely labeled images that can be used in training of CNN-based semantic segmentation models, and King et al. \citep{king2018} demonstrated the strength of fully convolutional neural networks for semantic segmentation of underwater coral reef imagery. Several recent studies have implemented CNNs and FCNNs for underwater coral reef imagery segmentation, with varying numbers of semantic classes, ranging from binary coral/noncoral or live/dead coral classes \citep{zhang2022, kopecky2023, wang2026multiscale}, to classes for dominant coral genera and growth forms  \citep{zhang2024, li2024, lawson2025, zheng2025}, and training on photos collected during video and phototransect surveys and from online repositories. Image-level classification methods have also been explored for coral health monitoring: Macrohon and Hung applied a CNN to classify coral photos taken from various angles retrieved from the photosharing site Flickr, and reported 88\% accuracy on picture-wise classifications of bleached vs. healthy \citep{macrohonhung2025}, and Shao et al.\ \citep{shao2025multilabel} fine-tuned a DINOv2 vision foundation model for detecting coral health status from close-up images of corals taken during phototransects. Object detection models, such as YOLO models, have been applied to a number of marine applications (e.g., \citep{zhao2024rblyolo, zhao2025musselyolo}). Zheng et al. \citep{zheng2024} present CoralSCOP, which uses the Segment Anything Model \citep{kirillov2023segment} to generate masks and a parallel semantic branch to assign labels that can be defined by the user with additional training data. Here, we present 1) a novel pipeline that uses a similar parallel segmentation-labelling approach, combining Segment Anything~2 \citep{ravi2024sam2} to generate masks with an ensemble of CNN submodels to assign mask labels and 2) an application of semantic segmentation to automate classification of colony scale bleaching from underwater coral reef imagery, with 8 morpho-taxonomic classes, each further split into separate bleaching classes.
