< To prepare the data for machine learning, the raw annotations were consolidated into what the annotation team identified as eight suitable morpho-taxonomic classes: \emph{Branching Acropora}, \emph{Finger Acropora}, \emph{Finger Porites}, \emph{Knob Porites}, \emph{Mounding}, \emph{Pocillopora}, \emph{Table Acropora}, and an ``Oddball'' category for grouping uncommon morphologies. Each morpho-taxonomic class was further cross-classified by bleaching status (healthy or bleached). A separate `non-coral' class was also defined to capture negative examples such as sand, rubble, algae, or spurious segmentations. The `non-coral' class contains no hand-annotated masks at all: annotators were instructed only to outline live coral colonies, not to draw negative examples, so every `non-coral' training instance is instead synthetically constructed from SAM2 mask proposals with low overlap to any annotated coral colony (Section~\ref{sec:cnn}). The consolidation to seventeen classes reduced the parameter space for model training while preserving interpretability at the morpho-taxonomic level for reef monitoring. Collapsing the data into these classes does introduce the possibility of drowning out variations in abundance and bleaching rates among species within a class, but these decisions were constrained by the taxonomic granularity to which coral could be confidently identified by annotators and the scarcity of certain classes. Despite this, morphological class often remains a useful proxy for ecological function.
