< To construct a balanced and representative classification dataset, we first extract positive instances from ground truth masks and assign labels by morpho-taxonomic class and bleaching status. Negative instances are synthetically generated using the tuned SAM2 segmentation module in the previous section, selecting predicted masks with low overlap to ground truth annotations. These synthetic ``non-coral'' examples capture natural false positives from texture or color that resemble coral but lack biological structure. As demonstrated by Zheng et al.\ \citep{zheng2024}, these negative training data are important in preventing oversegmentation of non-coral features. The resulting dataset is represented as $\{(x_i, y_i)\}_{i=1}^N$, where $x_i \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times H \times W}$ denote a normalized image crop extracted around the proposed mask, where $C$ is the number of channels ($3$ for RGB), and $H = W = 128$ denote the height and width of the resized crop (a choice of resolution made to balance computational efficiency and feature information).
---
