< Finally, to mitigate class imbalance and ensure adequate representation of minority categories, we oversample each class to a minimum of $1{,}000$ training examples. In our dataset, the rarest class in our dataset comprises only $13$ instances, and the threshold of $1,000$ may be adjusted depending on the size and diversity of the available training data. For classes with insufficient samples, we perform sampling with replacement, augmenting each duplicated crop using a new realization of the stochastic transformation $\phi(\cdot) \sim \mathcal{A}$. This procedure helps balance the empirical class distribution and ensures that each synthetic instance is drawn from a diverse augmentation prior, enabling the classifier to better learn class-discriminative features under our limited data regime. Each classifier is trained on a bootstrapped sample of the training data to reduce computational burden per model while introducing controlled variability across ensemble members. This sampling variability encourages decorrelation among base learners, which is crucial for reducing the generalization error of the ensemble through variance reduction. Moreover, since each classifier is trained on a slightly different empirical distribution, the ensemble can better approximate posterior uncertainty and hedge against overfitting to specific training artifacts. Optimization is performed using the Adam optimizer, which adapts learning rates per parameter and accelerates convergence in high-dimensional settings. Each classifier is optimized using the standard categorical cross-entropy (CCE) loss, encouraging discriminative learning while enabling each submodel to serve as a weak learner in the ensemble.  In our latest generation, the training dataset comprised $N=58{,}716$ mask crops derived from 411 annotated images. Of these, 38,608 were synthetically generated ``non-coral'' negatives, while minority classes were oversampled to a minimum of 1,000 instances each, a strategy shown to improve CNN performance under imbalanced label distributions \citep{buda2018}.
