< Third, the segmentation stage remains both the bottleneck and the largest source of potential gains. Our mask consolidation algorithm balances recall of small objects with precision of large structures, but it remains an unsupervised procedure relying on tuned hyperparameters and grid-based prompt distributions. Performance is therefore sensitive to parameterization and limits reproducibility. The lowest-IoU cases are disproportionately scenes with sparse, degenerate coral cover---a handful of small live colonies scattered within a field of rubble and dead skeleton, visually similar enough to their surroundings that even expert annotators can disagree on their boundaries. One way to recover some of these missed instances is simply to run SAM2 with additional, independent prompt-grid configurations and merge the results, increasing the chance that at least one configuration proposes a mask over a small or camouflaged colony; however, since each additional configuration multiplies inference cost roughly linearly, this does not scale favorably to large surveys. A promising alternative would be a hybrid approach: precede SAM2 with a lightweight supervised detector (e.g., YOLOv8 \citep{yolov8}), using bounding boxes as proposals and SAM2 for mask refinement. Because such a detector is trained directly on the target objects, it remains a more promising direction for improving recall over simply adding more SAM2 passes. Such hybridization could mitigate false positives, reduce inference time, and provide a more supervised handle on segmentation. A related alternative, closer in spirit to CoralSCOP \citep{zheng2024}, would replace our dense grid-prompted SAM2 configuration with point prompting---querying SAM2 at a sparser set of learned or heuristically-placed points and applying our classification ensemble to the resulting masks---potentially reducing the number of redundant proposals that must be classified and consolidated per image.
