< Fourth, the computational demands of the modular pipeline pose challenges for real-time inference and scaling to large collections. The SAM2 module generates multiple mask proposals per image, taking 1--3 seconds per frame even with GPU utilization. Ensemble optimization requires repeated forward passes across CNNs, producing a concave accuracy--throughput tradeoff. Practitioners must therefore select a feasible ensemble size (e.g. with perhaps $M<5$) that balances performance with computational resources. A similar tradeoff governs test-time augmentation: because each CNN submodel was trained exclusively under the same stochastic augmentation used elsewhere in this pipeline, a single augmented crop is already a valid, in-distribution input at inference, but classification could in principle be stabilized further by classifying several independently augmented draws of the same mask and aggregating (e.g.\ the modal prediction, or another stacked re-learner) rather than a single draw. We use one augmented draw per mask at inference throughout this paper for computational efficiency, and leave a systematic evaluation of this accuracy-compute tradeoff to future work.
